<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01X0">Remark <span data-tag="01X0">5.4.3.7</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-bicategory containing $X$ and $Y$. We will see later that the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}^{\mathrm{L}}( X, Y)$ is naturally equivalent to the <em>opposite</em> of the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}^{\mathrm{R}}( X, Y)$ (Proposition <a class="" data-content="This is a reference to something which is not yet available in Kerodon." data-html="true" data-placement="bottom" data-toggle="popover" data-trigger="focus" role="button" style="cursor: pointer;" tabindex="0" title="Future reference"><img src="/static/images/question.svg"/></a>). When $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is the Duskin nerve of a bicategory, we can do better: the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}^{\mathrm{L}}( X, Y)$ is <em>isomorphic</em> to the opposite of $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}^{\mathrm{R}}(X,Y)$; see Example <a data-tag="01L7" href="/tag/01L7">4.6.5.12</a>. </p>
